# Valgrind suppressions for the memory gate (make test-memory), each a cost
# of Chez Scheme itself, not of this binding. ADR-0003's amendment of
# 2026-10-09 records why each is here; check-memory-gate proves each still
# matches, and that none hides a leak the binding could make.
#
# A callback left without returning (#94). S_call_help, Chez's entry from C
# into Scheme, mallocs a jmp_buf for each call (312 B on aarch64 glibc,
# 200 B on x86-64) and frees it only when that call returns. A
# continuation that escapes a libuv callback never returns through it, and
# nor does an engine dropped while inside one, so the block is never freed,
# even when its thread ends. Nor is the block of a callback abandoned by a
# return into an older one: Chez drops it from its chain unfreed
# (.plans/escape-costs-mutation-log.md). A child forked before exec
# reports the block its parent held at the fork.
# Valgrind calls the block "still reachable" or "definitely lost" by how
# collections happened to run: test-loop-claim's escaped teardown flipped
# from one to the other on a change to an unrelated library. A block the
# binding allocates comes from its shim's calloc, and one from foreign-alloc
# from s_malloc, never from malloc called directly by S_call_help, so
# neither matches. Every kind is matched, so that check-memory-gate's
# escape mode can count the suppressed blocks; only definite leaks gate.
{
   chez-escaped-callback-jmp-buf
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,possible,reachable
   fun:malloc
   fun:S_call_help
}
